well there are plenty of frontline  healthcare workers who are still  hesitant about getting the vaccine in  fact an estimated  29 of all health care workers are really  having second thoughts about getting  that shot  so here's a look now at some of the  incentives that are being offered to  change people's minds  they've seen first hand the horrors of  the pandemic  still some health care workers are  hesitant about cobit 19 vaccines  but public health officials are working  to address their concerns about vaccine  safety my primary  message to health care professionals is  please get vaccinated  another barrier a mistrust of government  and medicine among some minorities  who make up about 40 percent of u.s  health care workers  stemming in part from our country's  history of dishonest medical experiments  on people of color jennifer stevenson is  an administrator at laredo hospital in  chicago when it comes to vaccines we've  been like the guinea pigs  jennifer did end up coming around then  this week  got her second dose what was effective  for me was actually  not only someone sitting down and  talking to me but actually  seeing leadership stepping up to the  plate and actually taking the vaccine  the hospital added a paid day off as a  reward  institutions across the country are  coming up with creative ways to convince  their staff to take the shot  houston methodist offers a 500 cash  incentive  and an atlanta nursing home chain  raffles off bonuses vacation  days and free tvs  now experts say more public health  education is really critical in order  for us to overcome this vaccine  hesitancy  both in health care workers and in the  general population
 

